Classical swine fever can affect any animal, regardless of age. As a rule, if a farm is exposed to the disease, almost 70% of the pigs die. After a major outbreak, large sums must be spent on disinfecting premises, removing animals, and implementing quarantine measures. To reduce costs, it is advisable to implement preventive measures to prevent the disease and detect the plague in its early stages. This type of disease has been given the following name - classical swine fever. This was done to distinguish the disease from a more dangerous type - African swine fever.

General characteristics of the disease
As you know, the study of this disease first began in the USA in 1830-1833. It was proven that the plague in pigs was of viral origin in 1903. By this period, signs of swine fever had begun to appear in other European countries via sea routes. Classical plague was first discovered on the territory of Russia in 1893.

Initially, scientists believed that the disease was caused by the agent B. Suipestifer. Later, however, American scientists showed that the presence of this dangerous microorganism in animals was the result of paratyphoid fever. The pathogen appears in the body of animals that are exposed to this disease.

Causative agent
It is known that classical plague is caused by a virus that contains RNA and is excreted by many sick animals. It is important to note that in the photograph, not only are the manifestations of classical plague in pigs studied, but it is also taken into account that this virus has a high degree of resistance to environmental conditions. The plague virus can survive on pig farms for 12 months.

The virus is also found in the food of slaughtered animals:
- 300 days in beef;
- 2 years or more in frozen meat products.

Classical plague has several types of viruses, including:
- type A - can cause an acute form of the disease;
- type B is a pathology that can develop into atypical and chronic forms;
- type C - in this case, the disease proceeds without visible symptoms, and this type is usually used to prepare a vaccine.

Infection can occur through inhalation or through skin lesions. Once the virus enters the animal's body, it begins to affect the liver and bone marrow.

Attention! The virus can be inactivated by heating to +80°C.

In the photograph below, you can see the signs of swine fever.

How dangerous is the disease?
In addition to studying the signs of plague in pigs in the photograph, you need to know how dangerous this disease is from scientific literature. In this case, it should be understood that swine fever is not dangerous for humans. However, since the pathogens of the plague and its varieties are not yet well understood, it is advisable not to consume the meat of infected animals. The virus that infects pigs can mutate and persist in meat products for 150 to 300 days.

Since classical plague is detected in later stages and can no longer be treated in this state, veterinarians remove infected individuals and all animals that were in close proximity to sick pigs when an epidemic is detected. For farms, classical plague is a real disaster, as it affects almost all livestock.

Forms and symptoms of classical swine fever in pigs
It is important to have information about the general symptoms and treatment of plague in pigs, as well as about the existing forms of this type of disease. As you know, classical plague can occur in pigs in the following forms:
- lightning fast;
- acute;
- subacute;
- chronic.

If you understand how these forms differ from each other and how to recognize them, you can prevent a massive infection of animals on a pig farm.

Lightning plague
As a rule, this form occurs only in suckling piglets. During infection, you can observe fever, depression of the animal, and vomiting. In some cases, piglets may fall into a coma or begin to have convulsions. Practice shows that death occurs 24 to 48 hours after the appearance of the first signs of the disease.

Acute form of swine fever
The following symptoms are characteristic of this form:
- hyperthermia with an elevated body temperature of up to 40.5-42°C;
- after 2-3 days, the animals begin to slowly lose their appetite;
- pigs have a constant feeling of thirst;
- over time, chills appear;
- vomiting;
- hemorrhagic diarrhea replaces constipation;
- conjunctivitis appears, the eyelids begin to swell and stick together due to a large release of pus;
- the back arches, the animals lie down in a bed;
- paresis of the hind limbs appears;
- the urine becomes darker;
- small blisters filled with yellow liquid can be seen on the skin;
- in addition, bleeding can be observed on the surface of the skin, which merge. After a certain time, the skin becomes purple. If you press, the spots will not disappear;
- nasal bleeding develops;
- damage to the heart occurs;
- the ears, tail, and penis turn blue;
- before the onset of death, hypothermia develops with a temperature of 35-36°C.

In young pigs, the acute form of classical plague lasts 7-10 days, and in sows, abortions are observed.

Subacute form of plague in pigs
In this case, the disease can occur in several forms:
- intestinal;
- pulmonary.

It is important to understand that the subacute form develops in animals that have developed resistance to the plague agent. The period of the disease can last from 2 to 3 weeks.

In the picture below, you can see a weakened animal with signs of classical swine fever in pigs.

Intestinal form of plague
In animals, enterocolitis appears, which is characterized by fever in pigs and delayed stools. When the condition changes to diarrhea, there is a loss of appetite.

Pulmonary form of plague
In this form of the disease in pigs, pneumonia develops, which causes severe coughing and difficulty breathing. To reduce the level of pain, the animals sit like dogs. If animals have pneumonia, it can be fatal.

Chronic plague
As a rule, the chronic form of classical plague occurs on pig farms where pigs are vaccinated, but there are some violations in the standards of care and feeding. First, all the animals become weakened. The duration of the disease can last from 60 days or more. In infected animals, the following signs of classical plague can be observed:
- severe cough;
- interruptions in appetite - at first it disappears completely, and after a while it reappears;
- eczema appears;
- the animals look exhausted.

The chronic form of classical plague usually weakens the animal, making pigs more susceptible to paratyphoid and pasteurellosis.

Attention! If an animal survives the disease, it acquires immunity to classical plague for a period of 12 months.

Signs of swine fever during necropsy
After the infected animal dies and no signs of the disease are detected, an autopsy is usually performed. In this case, classical plague in pigs can be identified by the following characteristics:
- there are many hemorrhages on the skin, which differ in configuration and size;
- the lymph nodes are hypertrophied, have a dark blood-colored hue, and you can see marbling in the section;
- spots are found on the lungs;
- during the autopsy, hemorrhages can be found on the heart;
- if you look at the signs of the disease in pigs in the photograph after opening, you can see that there are hemorrhages on the kidneys in the form of small bright spots;
- if death occurs in the subacute form, then buboes characteristic of plague can also be found on the intestinal mucosa.

Thanks to the autopsy, classical swine fever in pigs can be detected, which eliminates African plague (the most dangerous), erysipelas, and pasteurellosis.

Is it possible to treat plague in pigs?
It is important to understand that the survival rate in classical swine fever is low, which makes the disease much more dangerous. If it is necessary to destroy the plague virus, it is guaranteed that only increasing the temperature will help. The virus dies immediately in the process of boiling and at a temperature of +75-80°C after a few hours.

To remove the virus, it is recommended to use a 2% solution of formaldehyde and a solution of bleach. It is possible to treat pigs only in the early stages, but in the future, the number of deaths can range from 60% to 100%.

Control measures
In the case when signs of plague in pigs are detected in the early stages, treatment with hyperimmune serum should be carried out immediately. As practice shows, the effectiveness of the drug depends primarily on the stage of the disease.

Today, every farmer has the opportunity to purchase a developed pharmacological complex of drugs, with which it is possible to save the maximum number of livestock. The most important thing is to understand and identify classical plague in time, and then take immediate measures to treat the infected animals.

Preventive measures
To prevent such a disease on a farm, it is recommended to use preventive measures:
- protect the farm from attacks by wild animals;
- disinfect the premises in which pigs are kept in a timely manner;
- new animals should be quarantined before being placed in the herd;
- heat-treat food to disinfect water.

The main method by which it is possible to prevent many infectious diseases, including classical swine fever in pigs, is vaccination. The most effective are complex vaccines, which have shown their best side in the process of research.

Adults are recommended to be vaccinated every 10-12 months, and piglets should be injected at 50 and 75 days of age with 2 ml of the preparation. It is recommended to start vaccinating sows 20 days before the expected mating.

Attention! The drug should be used within 3-4 hours after preparation.

Conclusion
Classical swine fever is not dangerous for humans, but it poses a particular danger to farms. This is due to the fact that the mortality rate can vary from 60 to 100%, so the pig farm will suffer significant damage. To prevent the onset of the disease, it is recommended to take preventive measures in a timely manner.

